$DIR/main.p:12:37: cannot convert 3 (untyped int constant) to owner type sys.Fd outside package sys
    An owner owns memory or another resource and releases it when it is dropped,
    so it moves instead of being copied. Converting a plain untyped int into a
    sys.Fd would make a second owner of whatever the value refers to (a
    descriptor, a page frame), and both would release it. Only the package that
    declares sys.Fd may do that, where its invariants are kept.
    Use a constructor from the package that declares sys.Fd.
    (see "Conversions" in the Plutonium spec, section 11.24)
    (run "pluto explain E3212" for a longer explanation)

$DIR/main.p:14:43: cannot convert f (value of int32 type Fake) to owner type sys.Fd outside package sys
    An owner owns memory or another resource and releases it when it is dropped,
    so it moves instead of being copied. Converting the owner Fake into a sys.Fd
    moves it, but the result is a sys.Fd that nobody in its package made. Fake
    is made by the rules of another package, perhaps from any number, so the
    conversion could forge a sys.Fd. Only the package that declares sys.Fd may
    do that, where its invariants are kept, or the package of a type defined
    from sys.Fd.
    Use a constructor from the package that declares sys.Fd.
    (see "Conversions" in the Plutonium spec, section 11.24)
    (run "pluto explain E3212" for a longer explanation)

$DIR/main.p:16:53: cannot convert fd (value of int32 type sys.Fd) to owner type sys.Other outside package sys
    An owner owns memory or another resource and releases it when it is dropped,
    so it moves instead of being copied. Converting the owner sys.Fd into a
    sys.Other moves it, but the result is a sys.Other that nobody in its package
    made. sys.Fd is made by the rules of another package, perhaps from any
    number, so the conversion could forge a sys.Other. Only the package that
    declares sys.Other may do that, where its invariants are kept, or the
    package of a type defined from sys.Other.
    Use a constructor from the package that declares sys.Other.
    (see "Conversions" in the Plutonium spec, section 11.24)
    (run "pluto explain E3212" for a longer explanation)

$DIR/main.p:18:53: cannot convert n (value of type *int32) to *sys.Fd: the pointer gives write access to an owner of type sys.Fd outside package sys
    An owner owns memory or another resource and releases it when it is dropped,
    so it moves instead of being copied. A pointer conversion from *int32 to
    *sys.Fd lets code read and write the same object as either type. A write
    through it could make a sys.Fd from a plain value, or replace one, so the
    conversion is allowed only where int32 converts to sys.Fd and sys.Fd
    converts to int32. Only the package that declares sys.Fd may do that, where
    its invariants are kept.
    Use the functions and methods of the package that declares sys.Fd.
    (see "Conversions" in the Plutonium spec, section 11.24)
    (run "pluto explain E3212" for a longer explanation)

